1
00:00:00,103 --> 00:00:04,526
Before You Share a Claim A dramatic video can spread across a

2
00:00:04,539 --> 00:00:09,987
network before anybody examines its origin A shadowy account may distort an

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,789
incident attach a false caption and provoke indignation When a striking image

4
00:00:15,803 --> 00:00:20,026
confirms our existing opinion the impulse to share it can be overwhelming

5
00:00:20,934 --> 00:00:26,908
Nevertheless popularity is not evidence of accuracy Our journalism workshop taught a

6
00:00:26,921 --> 00:00:33,053
practical sequence First identify the original author and the date of publication

7
00:00:33,961 --> 00:00:39,829
Next compare the report with independent accounts Finally ask whether a missing

8
00:00:39,842 --> 00:00:45,263
detail could alter the interpretation We learnt to distinguish a documented occurrence

9
00:00:45,276 --> 00:00:50,276
from gossip an imitation from an authentic recording and criticism from slander

10
00:00:51,184 --> 00:00:55,763
A responsible editor must not fabricate testimony or disguise a sham interview

11
00:00:55,776 --> 00:01:00,408
as factual reporting Had the first readers paused to check the source

12
00:01:00,553 --> 00:01:05,684
the rumour might not have spread so widely Verification can seem slow

13
00:01:05,895 --> 00:01:11,632
particularly during a crisis but an inaccurate message can magnify fear The

14
00:01:11,645 --> 00:01:15,895
aim is neither to suppress disagreement nor to censor every unusual opinion

15
00:01:16,803 --> 00:01:20,974
It is to make public discussion more informed rational and accountable
